     expression                       result          note
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     0.5 + 0.25 == 0.75               True            halves and quarters are exact in binary
     0.1 * 3 == 0.3                   False           0.30000000000000004 is the next double up
     math.isclose(0.1 * 3, 0.3)       True            one gap apart, far inside rel_tol=1e-09
     math.isclose(1e-20, 0.0)         False           relative to zero is zero; abs_tol is 0.0
     float('nan') in [float('nan')]   False           two objects, so == decides, and says no
     min(float('nan'), 0.0)           nan             asks 0.0 < nan: False, so nan stays
     2.0 ** 1024                      OverflowError   ** checks for overflow; * does not
     -0.0 == 0.0                      True            different bits, equal numbers

     THE ONE THAT DEPENDS ON THE ORDER
     Line 6. Swapped, min(0.0, float('nan')) is 0.0. min() only
     replaces its answer when a later argument compares less, and
     every comparison against a NaN is False, so whichever argument
     comes first is kept. A minimum should not depend on the order
     it was asked in, and with a NaN in it, it does.

     THE TWO WHERE == WAS THE RIGHT TEST
     Lines 1 and 8. 0.5, 0.25, 0.75 and both zeros are stored exactly,
     so == compares the numbers you wrote. Line 2 is the same shape
     as line 1 and is False, because 0.1 and 0.3 are not stored
     exactly -- which is the whole reason line 3 exists.
